// Compiled by bin/build-help.php from help/*.md (the add-on's topics only). Do not edit.
export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"civilcraft","addon":"wastewater","name":"Water and wastewater treatment","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"wastewater-site","scope":"addon:wastewater","title":"Water treatment: size a plant and put its basins on the site","summary":"Run the activated sludge calculator (the published ASM1 model) or the design equations beside your site, then draw each basin's footprint on the plan from its volume and water depth.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["water treatment","wastewater","activated sludge","asm1","clarifier","secondary settler","sludge blanket","takacs","temperature","bsm1","benchmark","disinfection","basin","footprint","site plan","aeration tank","watertap"],"headings":[{"id":"size-the-plant","text":"Size the plant","level":2},{"id":"the-clarifier-the-sludge-blanket-and-the-water-temperature","text":"The clarifier, the sludge blanket and the water temperature","level":2},{"id":"check-it-against-the-benchmark","text":"Check it against the benchmark","level":2},{"id":"put-the-basins-on-the-site","text":"Put the basins on the site","level":2},{"id":"other-engines","text":"Other engines","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAdd the add-on first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA study aid, not an engineering approval. The ASM1 equations and parameters were compared with the published BSM1 report and the benchmark&#039;s steady state is reproduced by our tests, but no engineer has checked them yet; every result says so. ASM3 and ASM2d, the anaerobic digester, membranes and chemical dosing have their own calculators in the same tab: see the topic on water treatment models.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"size-the-plant\"\u003ESize the plant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWater treatment\u003C/span\u003E tab in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActivated sludge plant, screens, clarifier and disinfection\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActivated sludge: tank size, return ratio, clarifier\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEdit the sample input (plain JSON): the tanks and their volumes in m3, the flows in m3/d, the influent, and under \u003Ccode\u003Esite\u003C/code\u003E the water depth in metres (one number, or one per tank such as \u003Ccode\u003E{&quot;A1&quot;: 4, &quot;O1&quot;: 6}\u003C/code\u003E) and the gap between basins.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The results come as a table: the steady state, the COD and nitrogen balances (they must close), the effluent, the sludge age, and one line per basin with its area. Anything to check is listed under the table and in Problems.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-clarifier-the-sludge-blanket-and-the-water-temperature\"\u003EThe clarifier, the sludge blanket and the water temperature\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sample input is the plant of the published benchmark BSM1 (Alex and others, IWA task group, Lund University, 2008): five tanks, the internal recycle, the sludge return and its secondary clarifier.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Ccode\u003Esettler\u003C/code\u003E give the clarifier&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003Earea\u003C/code\u003E (m2), \u003Ccode\u003Eheight\u003C/code\u003E (m), the number of \u003Ccode\u003Elayers\u003C/code\u003E and the \u003Ccode\u003EfeedLayer\u003C/code\u003E counted from the bottom. \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;settler&quot;: {}\u003C/code\u003E is the BSM1 clarifier: 1,500 m2, 4 m, ten layers, fed at layer 6. Solids settle by the double-exponential velocity of Takacs, Patry and Nolasco (1991) with the parameters printed in the BSM1 report (its Table 4); change them under \u003Ccode\u003Esettler.params\u003C/code\u003E if you have your own sludge&#039;s.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter a run the panel says the height of the sludge blanket, the effluent solids and the underflow solids, and draws the layers from the bottom up. The blanket is the layers, from the bottom, whose solids are at or above 3,000 g/m3 (the BSM1 threshold); set \u003Ccode\u003Esettler.blanketAt\u003C/code\u003E for another value. A blanket that reaches the feed layer is a warning: the clarifier is overloaded at those flows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo keep the older, simpler clarifier, remove \u003Ccode\u003Esettler\u003C/code\u003E and give \u003Ccode\u003Ecapture\u003C/code\u003E, the share of solids it keeps (0 to 1).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E is the water temperature in degrees C. The growth, decay, hydrolysis and ammonification rates are corrected from the BSM1 set (about 15 C) with the law the BSM2 benchmark uses, through its values at 10 C and 15 C; outside 10 to 15 C the result warns that the rates are extrapolated. Add \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;soSatFromT&quot;: true\u003C/code\u003E to correct the oxygen saturation too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-it-against-the-benchmark\"\u003ECheck it against the benchmark\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBSM1 benchmark: steady state against the published table\u003C/span\u003E and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. It builds the BSM1 plant and lists every value of the report&#039;s published steady state (reactors, clarifier, effluent, sludge age) beside this run, with how many lie within the tolerance: 0.1 % of the published value or 0.001 g/m3, whichever is larger.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-basins-on-the-site\"\u003EPut the basins on the site\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter a run of the activated sludge calculator, press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the basins on the site\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach tank becomes a closed feature line (use &quot;off&quot;: linework, not part of the ground surface), named with its volume and depth, all in one undo step. The footprint is a square of the right area, in one row from west to east: the shape and the place are yours to change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe basins go to the middle of the points and lines already in the project. To choose the place, add \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;at&quot;: [x, y]\u003C/code\u003E (project units, the south-west corner of the first basin) under \u003Ccode\u003Esite\u003C/code\u003E and run again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESite plan (GeoJSON)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESite plan (DXF)\u003C/span\u003E save the same footprints in metres, for another program or for Import.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"other-engines\"\u003EOther engines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWaterTAP is listed as an engine it could use one day. Its licence (BSD-3-Clause with a grant-back clause for enhancements, read from its own licence file) waits on the owner&#039;s decision, and no script for it ships, so the panel says it is not offered. It would only ever run as a program installed on your own computer, through CadCraft Studio, never inside CadCraft.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Add the add-on first. A study aid, not an engineering approval. The ASM1 equations and parameters were compared with the published BSM1 report and the benchmark's steady state is reproduced by our tests, but no engineer has checked them yet; every result says so. ASM3 and ASM2d, the anaerobic digester, membranes and chemical dosing have their own calculators in the same tab: see the topic on water treatment models. Size the plant Open the Water treatment tab in the right panel. Pick Activated sludge plant, screens, clarifier and disinfection or Activated sludge: tank size, return ratio, clarifier. Edit the sample input (plain JSON): the tanks and their volumes in m3, the flows in m3/d, the influent, and under site the water depth in metres (one number, or one per tank such as {\"A1\": 4, \"O1\": 6}) and the gap between basins. Press Run. The results come as a table: the steady state, the COD and nitrogen balances (they must close), the effluent, the sludge age, and one line per basin with its area. Anything to check is listed under the table and in Problems. The clarifier, the sludge blanket and the water temperature The sample input is the plant of the published benchmark BSM1 (Alex and others, IWA task group, Lund University, 2008): five tanks, the internal recycle, the sludge return and its secondary clarifier. Under settler give the clarifier's area (m2), height (m), the number of layers and the feedLayer counted from the bottom. \"settler\": {} is the BSM1 clarifier: 1,500 m2, 4 m, ten layers, fed at layer 6. Solids settle by the double-exponential velocity of Takacs, Patry and Nolasco (1991) with the parameters printed in the BSM1 report (its Table 4); change them under settler.params if you have your own sludge's. After a run the panel says the height of the sludge blanket, the effluent solids and the underflow solids, and draws the layers from the bottom up. The blanket is the layers, from the bottom, whose solids are at or above 3,000 g/m3 (the BSM1 threshold); set settler.blanketAt for another value. A blanket that reaches the feed layer is a warning: the clarifier is overloaded at those flows. To keep the older, simpler clarifier, remove settler and give capture, the share of solids it keeps (0 to 1). T is the water temperature in degrees C. The growth, decay, hydrolysis and ammonification rates are corrected from the BSM1 set (about 15 C) with the law the BSM2 benchmark uses, through its values at 10 C and 15 C; outside 10 to 15 C the result warns that the rates are extrapolated. Add \"soSatFromT\": true to correct the oxygen saturation too. Check it against the benchmark Pick BSM1 benchmark: steady state against the published table and press Run. It builds the BSM1 plant and lists every value of the report's published steady state (reactors, clarifier, effluent, sludge age) beside this run, with how many lie within the tolerance: 0.1 % of the published value or 0.001 g/m3, whichever is larger. Put the basins on the site After a run of the activated sludge calculator, press Put the basins on the site. Each tank becomes a closed feature line (use \"off\": linework, not part of the ground surface), named with its volume and depth, all in one undo step. The footprint is a square of the right area, in one row from west to east: the shape and the place are yours to change. The basins go to the middle of the points and lines already in the project. To choose the place, add \"at\": [x, y] (project units, the south-west corner of the first basin) under site and run again. Site plan (GeoJSON) and Site plan (DXF) save the same footprints in metres, for another program or for Import. Other engines WaterTAP is listed as an engine it could use one day. Its licence (BSD-3-Clause with a grant-back clause for enhancements, read from its own licence file) waits on the owner's decision, and no script for it ships, so the panel says it is not offered. It would only ever run as a program installed on your own computer, through CadCraft Studio, never inside CadCraft."},{"slug":"wastewater-models","scope":"addon:wastewater","title":"Water treatment models: ASM3, ASM2d, digester, membranes and dosing","summary":"Choose the activated sludge model (ASM1, ASM3 or ASM2d), run an anaerobic digester on the plant's waste sludge, size an MBR or a reverse osmosis stage, and work out chemical doses, with every source named.","section":"tasks","order":111,"keywords":["water treatment","wastewater","activated sludge","asm1","asm3","asm2d","phosphorus","bio-p","pao","anaerobic digestion","adm1","bsm2","biogas","methane","digester","membrane bioreactor","mbr","flux","reverse osmosis","ro","desalination","solution-diffusion","ferric chloride","alum","lime","caustic","alkalinity","chlorine","hypochlorite","dosing"],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-activated-sludge-model","text":"Choose the activated sludge model","level":2},{"id":"run-a-digester","text":"Run a digester","level":2},{"id":"size-a-membrane-bioreactor","text":"Size a membrane bioreactor","level":2},{"id":"size-a-reverse-osmosis-stage","text":"Size a reverse osmosis stage","level":2},{"id":"work-out-chemical-doses","text":"Work out chemical doses","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAdd the add-on first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA study aid, not an engineering approval. The ADM1 digester reproduces the published BSM2 steady state; ASM3 and ASM2d come from open programs that cite the IWA papers, not from the papers themselves; the membrane and dosing calculators use the coefficients you type. No engineer has checked any of them yet, and every result says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-activated-sludge-model\"\u003EChoose the activated sludge model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWater treatment\u003C/span\u003E tab in the right panel and pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActivated sludge with a model choice: ASM1, ASM3 or ASM2d\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the list under it pick ASM1, ASM3 or ASM2d. The input box fills with a sample plant for that model and it runs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEdit the tanks, flows, influent and clarifier capture (plain JSON) and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The table shows the steady state, the COD and nitrogen balances (and phosphorus for ASM2d), the effluent, the sludge age, and the worst row of the model&#039;s own continuity check. Each must be close to zero.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EASM3\u003C/strong\u003E stores substrate before growth. Its influent names are SI, SS, XI, XS, XH, XSTO, XA, SNH4, SN2, SNOX, SALK and XSS (left out, XSS is worked out from the particulate COD). Give \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E between 10 and 20 C: the rates move between the model&#039;s two temperature columns. The values come from WaterTAP&#039;s ASM3 and agree with the Modelica WasteWater library, except the anoxic decay of nitrifiers, which that library writes ten times larger (0.5 per day). 0.05 is used here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EASM2d\u003C/strong\u003E adds biological phosphorus removal (phosphate-accumulating organisms, polyphosphate, PHA, denitrifying PAOs), fermentation and a metal hydroxide for chemical phosphorus. Put an unaerated tank first so the PAOs can take up acetate, and keep nitrate out of it: the internal recycle returns to the first tank, so the sample has none (with it, phosphate is no longer removed). The values come from QSDsan. ASM2d cannot use the layered settler: give a capture.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe &quot;?&quot; coefficients of each matrix (oxygen, ammonium, phosphate, alkalinity) are not typed in. They are solved from the conservation of COD, nitrogen, phosphorus and charge, so every row balances.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-a-digester\"\u003ERun a digester\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnaerobic digester (ADM1 as published for BSM2): biogas and digested sludge\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the list, pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBSM2 test feed\u003C/span\u003E to run the published test case. The last line gives the largest difference from the published table, which is below one part in a hundred thousand. Pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWaste sludge from the activated sludge plant\u003C/span\u003E to digest the BSM1 plant&#039;s waste sludge, thickened by \u003Ccode\u003Esludge.thicken\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe results give the pH, the biogas flow at atmospheric pressure, its methane, carbon dioxide, hydrogen and water vapour, the methane flow and COD, and the COD, carbon and nitrogen balances.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sludge-to-digester step is this program&#039;s own simple interface, not the BSM2 interface: composites take the degradable COD, inerts the inert COD, and any organic nitrogen the composites cannot carry goes to ammonium so nitrogen is kept. Cations and anions set the pH; if you leave them out, the BSM2 test values are used and the result says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"size-a-membrane-bioreactor\"\u003ESize a membrane bioreactor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMembrane bioreactor: membrane area, permeate and retentate\u003C/span\u003E. Give the permeate flow (m3/d), the design flux (L/m2/h), the peak factor, the retentate recycle (as a multiple of the permeate) and the mixed liquor solids (g/L). The table gives the membrane area at the average and at the peak flow, the flows, and the solids in the retentate.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERanges read: a sustainable long-term flux of 15 to 30 L/m2/h, MLSS of 8 to 15 g/L, TMP about 20 kPa, HRT 1 to 9 h and sludge age above 20 days for submerged MBRs (TU Delft lecture notes, 2012). Peaks above 1.5 to 2 times the average design flow need more membrane or equalization (US EPA fact sheet EPA 832-F-07-015). A value outside these ranges is a warning, never a refusal.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"size-a-reverse-osmosis-stage\"\u003ESize a reverse osmosis stage\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse osmosis: solution-diffusion flux, recovery, permeate and concentrate\u003C/span\u003E. Give the feed flow and salt concentration, the recovery, the membrane&#039;s water and salt permeabilities \u003Ccode\u003ELp\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EBs\u003C/code\u003E, the osmotic coefficient \u003Ccode\u003Efos\u003C/code\u003E (bar per g/L) and either the area or a design flux. The table gives the permeate and concentrate flows and salt, the rejection, the osmotic pressure at the membrane and the pressure the feed needs. Set \u003Ccode\u003EmaxPressure\u003C/code\u003E to your pump&#039;s or pressure vessel&#039;s limit to be warned when it is exceeded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sample values are one seawater element&#039;s (Li and Li, 2025): Lp 1.06 L/m2/h/bar, Bs 0.044 L/m2/h, about 26 bar osmotic pressure at 35 g/L. Brackish-water membranes are different. Use your membrane&#039;s data sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"work-out-chemical-doses\"\u003EWork out chemical doses\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChemical dosing: phosphorus precipitation, alkalinity, chlorine\u003C/span\u003E. Fill in any of the three parts:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ephosphorus\u003C/code\u003E: flow, soluble phosphate, the molar ratio (mol of iron or aluminium per mol of phosphate) and \u003Ccode\u003Efe\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Eal\u003C/code\u003E. 1.0 is the stoichiometric dose; 1.5 to 2.0 removes 80 to 98 % of soluble phosphorus, and about 6 to 7 is needed below 0.1 mg/L (US EPA Nutrient Control Design Manual, 2010). Give the product strength (ferric chloride is typically 37 to 47 %, alum 48 %) and your supplier&#039;s density to get the daily volume.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ealkalinity\u003C/code\u003E: nitrogen nitrified and denitrified, the feed alkalinity and the residual to keep (all in mg/L as CaCO3), and \u003Ccode\u003Elime\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Ecaustic\u003C/code\u003E. Nitrification uses 7.14 and denitrification gives back 3.57 mg CaCO3 per mg N (same manual).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Echlorine\u003C/code\u003E: demand plus residual gives the dose. Give the hypochlorite strength and density from your supplier for the volume.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo density or hypochlorite strength is ever assumed: without them the dry mass is given and the result says what is missing. The list under the calculator switches between ferric chloride, alum and caustic soda.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Add the add-on first. A study aid, not an engineering approval. The ADM1 digester reproduces the published BSM2 steady state; ASM3 and ASM2d come from open programs that cite the IWA papers, not from the papers themselves; the membrane and dosing calculators use the coefficients you type. No engineer has checked any of them yet, and every result says so. Choose the activated sludge model Open the Water treatment tab in the right panel and pick Activated sludge with a model choice: ASM1, ASM3 or ASM2d. In the list under it pick ASM1, ASM3 or ASM2d. The input box fills with a sample plant for that model and it runs. Edit the tanks, flows, influent and clarifier capture (plain JSON) and press Run. The table shows the steady state, the COD and nitrogen balances (and phosphorus for ASM2d), the effluent, the sludge age, and the worst row of the model's own continuity check. Each must be close to zero. ASM3 stores substrate before growth. Its influent names are SI, SS, XI, XS, XH, XSTO, XA, SNH4, SN2, SNOX, SALK and XSS (left out, XSS is worked out from the particulate COD). Give T between 10 and 20 C: the rates move between the model's two temperature columns. The values come from WaterTAP's ASM3 and agree with the Modelica WasteWater library, except the anoxic decay of nitrifiers, which that library writes ten times larger (0.5 per day). 0.05 is used here. ASM2d adds biological phosphorus removal (phosphate-accumulating organisms, polyphosphate, PHA, denitrifying PAOs), fermentation and a metal hydroxide for chemical phosphorus. Put an unaerated tank first so the PAOs can take up acetate, and keep nitrate out of it: the internal recycle returns to the first tank, so the sample has none (with it, phosphate is no longer removed). The values come from QSDsan. ASM2d cannot use the layered settler: give a capture. The \"?\" coefficients of each matrix (oxygen, ammonium, phosphate, alkalinity) are not typed in. They are solved from the conservation of COD, nitrogen, phosphorus and charge, so every row balances. Run a digester Pick Anaerobic digester (ADM1 as published for BSM2): biogas and digested sludge. In the list, pick BSM2 test feed to run the published test case. The last line gives the largest difference from the published table, which is below one part in a hundred thousand. Pick Waste sludge from the activated sludge plant to digest the BSM1 plant's waste sludge, thickened by sludge.thicken. The results give the pH, the biogas flow at atmospheric pressure, its methane, carbon dioxide, hydrogen and water vapour, the methane flow and COD, and the COD, carbon and nitrogen balances. The sludge-to-digester step is this program's own simple interface, not the BSM2 interface: composites take the degradable COD, inerts the inert COD, and any organic nitrogen the composites cannot carry goes to ammonium so nitrogen is kept. Cations and anions set the pH; if you leave them out, the BSM2 test values are used and the result says so. Size a membrane bioreactor Pick Membrane bioreactor: membrane area, permeate and retentate. Give the permeate flow (m3/d), the design flux (L/m2/h), the peak factor, the retentate recycle (as a multiple of the permeate) and the mixed liquor solids (g/L). The table gives the membrane area at the average and at the peak flow, the flows, and the solids in the retentate. Ranges read: a sustainable long-term flux of 15 to 30 L/m2/h, MLSS of 8 to 15 g/L, TMP about 20 kPa, HRT 1 to 9 h and sludge age above 20 days for submerged MBRs (TU Delft lecture notes, 2012). Peaks above 1.5 to 2 times the average design flow need more membrane or equalization (US EPA fact sheet EPA 832-F-07-015). A value outside these ranges is a warning, never a refusal. Size a reverse osmosis stage Pick Reverse osmosis: solution-diffusion flux, recovery, permeate and concentrate. Give the feed flow and salt concentration, the recovery, the membrane's water and salt permeabilities Lp and Bs, the osmotic coefficient fos (bar per g/L) and either the area or a design flux. The table gives the permeate and concentrate flows and salt, the rejection, the osmotic pressure at the membrane and the pressure the feed needs. Set maxPressure to your pump's or pressure vessel's limit to be warned when it is exceeded. The sample values are one seawater element's (Li and Li, 2025): Lp 1.06 L/m2/h/bar, Bs 0.044 L/m2/h, about 26 bar osmotic pressure at 35 g/L. Brackish-water membranes are different. Use your membrane's data sheet. Work out chemical doses Pick Chemical dosing: phosphorus precipitation, alkalinity, chlorine. Fill in any of the three parts: phosphorus: flow, soluble phosphate, the molar ratio (mol of iron or aluminium per mol of phosphate) and fe or al. 1.0 is the stoichiometric dose; 1.5 to 2.0 removes 80 to 98 % of soluble phosphorus, and about 6 to 7 is needed below 0.1 mg/L (US EPA Nutrient Control Design Manual, 2010). Give the product strength (ferric chloride is typically 37 to 47 %, alum 48 %) and your supplier's density to get the daily volume. alkalinity: nitrogen nitrified and denitrified, the feed alkalinity and the residual to keep (all in mg/L as CaCO3), and lime or caustic. Nitrification uses 7.14 and denitrification gives back 3.57 mg CaCO3 per mg N (same manual). chlorine: demand plus residual gives the dose. Give the hypochlorite strength and density from your supplier for the volume. No density or hypochlorite strength is ever assumed: without them the dry mass is given and the result says what is missing. The list under the calculator switches between ferric chloride, alum and caustic soda."}]};
